window.docContents[75].resolve({"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Cauchy-determinant-identity/#Chapter5___Lemma5___15___3":{"contents":"Lemma 5.15.3.\n\n\\frac{\\prod_{i<j}(z_j - z_i)(y_i - y_j)}{\\prod_{i,j}(z_i - y_j)} = \\det\\left(\\frac{1}{z_i - y_j}\\right).\n\nProof. Multiply both sides by \\prod_{i,j}(z_i - y_j). Then the right-hand side must vanish on the hyperplanes z_i = z_j and y_i = y_j (i.e., must be divisible by \\Delta(z)\\Delta(y)) and is a homogeneous polynomial of degree\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.15. The Frobenius character formula\tCauchy determinant identity","header":"Cauchy determinant identity","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Cauchy-determinant-identity/#Chapter5___Lemma5___15___3"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Extension-to-sl_LPAR_V_RPAR_-and-SL_LPAR_V_RPAR__SEMI_-recovery-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_-representation-theory/#Chapter5___Remark5___23___3":{"contents":"Remark 5.23.3. Since the Lie algebra \\mathfrak{sl}(V) of traceless operators on V is a quotient of \\mathfrak{gl}(V) by scalars, the above results extend in a straightforward manner to representations of the Lie algebra \\mathfrak{sl}(V). Similarly, the results for GL(V) extend to the case of the group SL(V) of operators with determinant 1. The only difference is that in this case the representations L_\\lambda and L_{\\lambda + 1^m} are isomorphic, so the irreducible representations are parametrized by integer sequences \\lambda_1 \\geq \\cdots \\geq \\lambda_N up to a simultaneous shift by a constant.\n\nIn particular, one can show that any finite dimensional representation of \\mathfrak{sl}(V) is completely reducible and any irreducible representation is of the form L_\\lambda (we will not do this here). For \\dim V = 2 one then recovers the representation theory of \\mathfrak{sl}(2) studied in Problem 2.15.1.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.23. Algebraic representations of $GL(V)$\tExtension to sl(V) and SL(V); recovery of sl(2) representation theory","header":"Extension to sl(V) and SL(V); recovery of sl(2) representation theory","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Extension-to-sl_LPAR_V_RPAR_-and-SL_LPAR_V_RPAR__SEMI_-recovery-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_-representation-theory/#Chapter5___Remark5___23___3"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Definition-6___4___3___-Root/#Chapter6___Definition6___4___3":{"contents":"Definition 6.4.3. A root with respect to a certain positive inner product is a shortest (with respect to this inner product) nonzero vector in \\mathbb{Z}^n.\n\nSo for the inner product B, a root is a nonzero vector x \\in \\mathbb{Z}^n such that\n\nB(x, x) = 2.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.4. Roots\tDefinition 6.4.3: Root","header":"Definition 6.4.3: Root","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Definition-6___4___3___-Root/#Chapter6___Definition6___4___3"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___24___-Problems/V____lambda-___-V_lambda-and-V_lambda-___-C_--___-V____lambda______/#Chapter5___Problem5___24___1":{"contents":"Problem 5.24.1. (a) Show that the S_n-representation\n\nV'_\\lambda := \\mathbb{C}[S_n] b_\\lambda a_\\lambda\n\nis isomorphic to V_\\lambda.\n\nHint: Define S_n-homomorphisms f : V_\\lambda \\to V'_\\lambda and g : V'_\\lambda \\to V_\\lambda by the formulas f(x) = x a_\\lambda and g(y) = y b_\\lambda, and show that they are inverse to each other up to a nonzero scalar.\n\n(b) Let \\phi : \\mathbb{C}[S_n] \\to \\mathbb{C}[S_n] be the automorphism sending s to (-1)^s s for any permutation s. Show that \\phi maps any representation V of S_n to V \\otimes \\mathbb{C}_-. Show also that \\phi(\\mathbb{C}[S_n] a) = \\mathbb{C}[S_n] \\phi(a), for a \\in \\mathbb{C}[S_n]. Use (a) to deduce that V_\\lambda \\otimes \\mathbb{C}_- = V_{\\lambda^*}, where \\lambda^* is the conjugate partition to \\lambda, obtained by reflecting the Young diagram of \\lambda.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.24. Problems\tV'_lambda ≅ V_lambda and V_lambda ⊗ C_- = V_{lambda*}","header":"V'_lambda ≅ V_lambda and V_lambda ⊗ C_- = V_{lambda*}","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___24___-Problems/V____lambda-___-V_lambda-and-V_lambda-___-C_--___-V____lambda______/#Chapter5___Problem5___24___1"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___4___-Equivalence-of-categories/Examples-of-equivalent-categories/#Chapter7___Discussion_after_Definition7___4___1":{"contents":"In particular, the above categories \\mathcal{C}_1 and \\mathcal{C}_2 are equivalent (check it!).\n\nAlso, the category FSet of finite sets is equivalent to the category whose objects are nonnegative integers, and morphisms are given by\n\n\\operatorname{Hom}(m, n) = \\operatorname{Maps}(\\{1, \\ldots, m\\}, \\{1, \\ldots, n\\}).\n\nAre these categories isomorphic? The answer to this question depends on whether you believe that there is only one finite set with a given number of elements, or that there are many of those. It seems better to think that there are many (without asking \"how many\"), so that isomorphic sets need not be literally equal, but this is really a matter of choice. In any case, this is not really a reasonable question; the answer to this question is irrelevant for any practical purpose, and thinking about it will give you nothing but a headache.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.4. Equivalence of categories\tExamples of equivalent categories","header":"Examples of equivalent categories","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___4___-Equivalence-of-categories/Examples-of-equivalent-categories/#Chapter7___Discussion_after_Definition7___4___1"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Ind___-Res___-Hom-as-additive-k-linear-functors/#Chapter7___Example7___9___2":{"contents":"Example 7.9.2. The functors \\operatorname{Ind}_K^G, \\operatorname{Res}_K^G, \\operatorname{Hom}_G(V, ?) in the theory of group representations over a field k are additive and k-linear.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.9. Exact functors\tInd, Res, Hom as additive k-linear functors","header":"Ind, Res, Hom as additive k-linear functors","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Ind___-Res___-Hom-as-additive-k-linear-functors/#Chapter7___Example7___9___2"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___9___-Problems/Section-6___9-heading___-Problems/#Chapter6___Section6___9_heading":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.9. Problems\tSection 6.9 heading: Problems","header":"Section 6.9 heading: Problems","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___9___-Problems/Section-6___9-heading___-Problems/#Chapter6___Section6___9_heading"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___20___-Historical-interlude___-Hermann-Weyl-at-the-intersection-of-limitation-and-freedom/#chapter-05___section-5-20":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results","header":"5.20. Historical interlude: Hermann Weyl at the intersection of limitation and freedom","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___20___-Historical-interlude___-Hermann-Weyl-at-the-intersection-of-limitation-and-freedom/#chapter-05___section-5-20"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/#chapter-08___section-8-1":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t8. Homological algebra","header":"8.1. Projective and injective modules","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/#chapter-08___section-8-1"}});
